  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE...Additional rounds of locally heavy thunderstorms could \nlead to instances of flash flooding through this afternoon.\n\nThe upper level low previously over the upper OH Valley continues to \nelongate/weaken as it moves northeast and away from our area this \nmorning. The associated frontal boundary over eastern OH / western \nPA early this morning is going to slowly drop south into our area by \nthis afternoon. The front is going to be the main focus for another \nround of showers and thunderstorms. The main uncertainty is the \nexact placement of this boundary, with most high res guidance \nshowing it from Baltimore to Staunton by 18Z this afternoon. Along \nand south/east of this line is where most of the rain chances (50-70 \npct) will be for today, with mostly dry conditions to the \nnorth/west. \n\nIt is still going to be warm and muggy areawide as highs reach the \nmid to upper 80s. Thunderstorms once again are going to be efficient \nrain makers with a deep warm cloud layer and PWATs above 2\". A few \ninstances of flash flooding are possible if any heavy thunderstorm \ndevelops and moves slowly over an urban or poor drainage area. The \nfrontal boundary completely crosses the area this evening, ushering \nin drier conditions for tonight. Slightly lower dew points allow \ntemps to drop to the 60s for most locations (upper 50s in the \nmountains). \n\nThe boundary attempts to lift north as a warm front Tuesday, but only \nmakes it to around I-64. This means most of the area remains dry. \nIsolated showers and thunderstorms are possible in the afternoon \nalong and south of a line from Staunton to Fredericksburg. The dry \nspell is going to be short lived as the warm front lifts north \nacross the entire area on Wednesday, bringing the return of an \nactive weather pattern that persists through the weekend.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Additional shower and thunderstorm chances\npersist through this weekend.\n\nShowers and thunderstorms will focus in the afternoon & evening \nhours each day. Increasing humidity will allow for PWATs ranging \nfrom the 75th-90th percentile, indicating potentially increased \nflooding concerns with any thunderstorms that do materialize. Severe \nthunderstorm probabilities continue to indicate the potential for \nsevere thunderstorms during the Thursday-Sunday timeframe, with \nmachine learning outputs remaining in the 15%-30% range. Still, that \nremains uncertain given the lack of defined synoptic support and \nabundant cloud cover over the region that limits instability.\n\nAs this synoptic pattern persists, temperatures gradually increase \nback to summertime normals, with elevated humidity contributing \ntowards heat indices that will approach 100F across the metros late \ninto the week. Looking ahead, deterministic models do show some \nsignal for this front transitioning offshore towards the start of \nnext week with drier air moving in from the west, potentially \nproviding some relief from the humidity. For now, the primary \nweather feature remains as humid & rainy, along with increasing \nheat.",
